WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:05.750
The Last Jedi may not be a film that's filled
to the brim with Easter eggs and clever secrets,

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.740
but it's full of callbacks and nods to obscure
aspects of Star Wars continuity.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.759
We may never get the answers to some of our
most pressing questions, but at least we know

00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:17.450
that as far as Star Wars is concerned, some
things never change.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:20.740
Here a few small details you may have missed
in The Last Jedi.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.050
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.920
Han's Dice

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.970
You may not have noticed them before, but
Han's golden dice finally get their moment

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:28.970
to shine.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:32.981
These dice first appeared in A New Hope, dangling
in the cockpit of the Millennium Falcon, but

00:00:32.981 --> 00:00:36.720
were never really seen clearly through the
rest of the trilogy.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.590
Nevertheless, they became so legendary that
they even got their own trading card, a mention

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:44.900
in a few Star Wars books, and even made the
news when it came to properly rebuilding the

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.870
Millennium Falcon in The Force Awakens.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:50.579
But because we've never actually seen these
things up close, it's always just been assumed

00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:53.239
that they're, well, basic human dice.

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:56.580
Turns out they're covered with alien symbols
instead of pips.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:01.010
Sorry, Chewie - those expensive replicas you
bought are now non-canon.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.010
Green harvest

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.790
Luke Skywalker's love of weird colored milk
is almost a central theme of the Star Wars

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:07.790
saga.

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:13.060
When he first appears on Tatooine as a lowly
moisture farmer, he's all about the blue milk,

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:14.720
which comes from Banthas.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.091
Blue milk even makes an appearance in Attack
of the Clones and Rogue One, so you can be

00:01:19.091 --> 00:01:24.010
sure that there are strong bones across that
entire far, far away galaxy.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:28.590
This time around, however, on his secluded
planet hideaway, Luke's getting into the green

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:29.590
stuff.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:33.920
In a weirdly gratuitous milking scene, Luke
takes it upon himself to squeeze some green

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.030
milk from an anatomically horrific animal
for himself.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.620
Breakfast of champions, Luke.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:45.710
The lonely island

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:50.299
There's a lot going on on the island that
housed the first Jedi temple, and not a lot

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:52.410
of it is actually addressed in The Last Jedi.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:56.450
Like, what's that weird dark side hole that
Rey is drawn into?

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.700
Luke made himself at home on the island after
Kylo Ren went berserk and burnt down Luke's

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:01.990
Jedi dojo.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:05.770
Luke had no intention of leaving the planet,
submerging his X-Wing off the coast of his

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.950
small island home, a visual echo of what happened
to the ship on Dagobah during his own training

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:11.950
with Yoda.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.920
And if there was any real question about where
Luke was going to end his journey, the answer

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.120
was in the planet's twin suns.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.150
In a powerful callback to the moment Luke
began to realize his purpose in A New Hope,

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:27.950
an aging Jedi Master Luke looks over the planet's
twin suns under the same swelling John Williams

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.340
score.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.650
Knights of Ren

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:35.950
The Force Awakens didn't do much to explain
Kylo Ren's band of evil weirdos.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.190
They're seen once in a vision, hyped up in
the press surrounding The Force Awakens…

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:41.760
and never seen again.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.739
One might think that Kylo Ren would have his
top dudes at his side while hunting down both

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:50.190
Rey and Luke, but he sticks to more military
and spiritual options instead.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:55.540
But at least we kind of finally know who they
are—or were—thanks to The Last Jedi.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.220
Kylo Ren's personal goth squad are the Jedi
from Luke's training academy, or at least

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.930
the ones who weren't killed by Kylo Ren himself.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:05.849
Why did they join Kylo, and where are they
now?

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:07.370
We may never know.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:08.930
Chop chop!

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.790
Star Wars has a well-documented love of chopping
off body parts, and why shouldn't it?

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:17.510
When you're swinging around laser swords that
cut through metal and rock like butter, you're

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.000
going to lose a few parts.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:25.160
And no villain lost their body parts more
unceremoniously than Darth Maul, a guy poised

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:29.250
to be a badass villain who gets chopped in
half and thrown down a space-hole after only

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.879
a few lines of dialogue.

00:03:30.879 --> 00:03:34.720
Maul's lines, by the way, were originally
going to be voiced by Benicio Del Toro, who

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.290
left The Phantom Menace project after most
of his dialogue was taken away.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:43.710
Of course, Del Toro makes his triumphantly
weird return in The Last Jedi as DJ.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.960
But back to being bisected by lightsabers.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:50.310
Snoke's unexpected death after doing a whole
lot of showboating brings back very clear

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:51.819
memories of Maul.

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:57.470
So let's all raise a glass to Supreme Leader
Snoke; he was around, he kinda did stuff,

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:01.330
he was ugly, and nobody really knows who he
was.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:06.170
But hey, if Darth Maul can come back with
a robot booty, anybody can.

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:07.780
Admiral Raddus

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.580
We know that nearly everyone who appeared
in Rogue One is dead, hero and villain alike,

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:17.759
and that includes Admiral Raddus, the Mon
Calamari officer of The Profundity, an iconic

00:04:17.759 --> 00:04:21.249
ship used in the battle to get the Death Star's
plans to the rebels.

00:04:21.249 --> 00:04:25.479
Yes, seeing a squid-headed alien at the helm
of a powerful ship was an obvious reference

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:30.360
to Admiral Ackbar's days of shouting "It's
a trap!" on the bridge of his own rebel ship,

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.819
but The Last Jedi took it just a little bit
deeper than that.

00:04:33.819 --> 00:04:39.069
The cumbersomely-named Star Wars: The Last
Jedi: The Visual Dictionary reveals a ton

00:04:39.069 --> 00:04:41.749
of information that the film never gets around
to.

00:04:41.749 --> 00:04:46.069
One of those things is that a major ship in
the battle scenes in The Last Jedi is called

00:04:46.069 --> 00:04:51.520
the Raddus, named as such at the behest of
Ackbar himself, in honor of the fish-face

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.219
that came before him.

00:04:53.219 --> 00:04:54.939
Celeb cameos

00:04:54.939 --> 00:04:59.710
Last but not least are the things that can
possibly, maybe be considered Easter eggs:

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.069
celebrity cameos.

00:05:01.069 --> 00:05:04.560
And just like in The Force Awakens, there
are a few to watch out for.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.800
Justin Theroux makes a very brief appearance
as the unnamed, gambling Master Codebreaker.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:14.009
Meanwhile, Rogue One director Gareth Edwards
makes an appearance as a resistance fighter.

00:05:14.009 --> 00:05:19.369
The massive army of bad guys include Game
of Thrones' Ralph Ineson as a bridge officer,

00:05:19.369 --> 00:05:24.110
along with fellow Thrones cast-member Kate
Dickie — both joining Captain Phasma actor

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:27.439
Gwendoline Christie in a galaxy far, far away.

00:05:27.439 --> 00:05:31.759
And Tom Hardy showed up as a Stormtrooper,
alongside musician Gary Barlow.

00:05:31.759 --> 00:05:35.559
Also rumored to be hidden in the film somewhere
are Joseph Gordon-Levitt and director Edgar

00:05:35.559 --> 00:05:36.559
Wright.

00:05:36.559 --> 00:05:41.960
And no Star Wars film would be complete without
a Warwick Davis appearance, this time as Wodibin,

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:44.000
a gambler on Canto Bight.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.310
Who else should you look for?

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:49.789
The cameo of Princes Harry and William, who
were NOT cut from the final film, despite

00:05:49.789 --> 00:05:53.059
what a joke by co-star John Boyega had implied
earlier.

00:05:53.059 --> 00:05:54.990
Quit messing around, Finn.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:56.520
"Droid, please!"

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:57.650
Thanks for watching!

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:00.099
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:00.099 --> 00:06:02.239
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

